.TH ELECTRIC-EYED 8 "October 2026" "electric-eye 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
electric-eyed \- eBPF system activity monitor daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B electric-eyed
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B electric-eyed
observes process lifecycle, file activity, TCP/UDP flows and evidence-backed
activity summaries using eBPF. It runs as an unprivileged system user with only
the capabilities needed for BPF/perf attachment, tracefs reads and
.B /proc/<pid>/exe
access.
.PP
The daemon exposes a read-only query API and a read-only MCP server over Unix
sockets. It does not freeze processes, block network traffic or modify package
state.
.SH RETENTION
Retention is configured independently for raw events, general activity,
process/exec history, flows, summaries and incidents. Heuristic summaries use
a shorter noise window. Incidents are retained per severity; high-severity
incident records retain their actor, journal and file evidence even after raw
events expire. See
.I config/default.toml
for the fields and defaults.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-stdout
Print observed events to stdout instead of only persisting them.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit JSON Lines and send diagnostics to stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-no-bpf
Run startup diagnostics and exit without loading BPF.
.TP
.B \-\-no-socket
Do not start the query API.
.TP
.B \-\-no-mcp
Do not start the MCP server.
.TP
.B \-\-no-db
Disable SQLite persistence.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
Override the query socket.
.TP
.BI \-\-mcp-socket " PATH"
Override the MCP socket.
.TP
.BI \-\-mcp-tools " PROFILE"
Select the MCP tool profile:
.BR default ,
.B all ,
or a comma-separated tool list. The default profile is the compact core set.
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Override the SQLite database path.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Load a TOML configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-\-ebpf-object " PATH"
Override the compiled eBPF object.
.TP
.B \-\-check-config
Validate configuration and exit without starting the daemon.
.TP
.B \-\-print-default-config
Print every supported configuration setting.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The daemon searches, in order:
.IP \(bu 2
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
.IP \(bu 2
.B ELECTRIC_EYE_CONFIG
.IP \(bu 2
.I ~/.config/electric-eye/config.toml
.IP \(bu 2
.I ~/.config/electriceye/config.toml
(legacy fallback)
.IP \(bu 2
.I /etc/electric-eye/config.toml
.PP
Explicitly selected files must exist. Unknown fields and invalid values fail
startup. See the electric-eye README and
.I config/default.toml
for every setting.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/electric-eye/electric-eye.sock
Read-only query socket.
.TP
.I /run/electric-eye/mcp.sock
Read-only MCP socket.
.TP
.I /var/lib/electric-eye/electric-eye.db
SQLite history.
.TP
.I /etc/electric-eye/config.toml
System configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/electric-eye/allowlist.conf
Legacy additional allowlist entries.
.SH SYSTEMD
The packaged unit is
.BR electric-eye.service .
Enable it with:
.PP
.nf
sudo systemctl enable --now electric-eye.service
.fi
.PP
Users who need CLI access must belong to the
.B electric-eye
group:
.PP
.nf
sudo usermod -aG electric-eye "$USER"
.fi
.PP
Log out and back in for the group change to take effect.
.SH PERFORMANCE
Ring loss, pending-syscall loss, identity loss and path uncertainty are reported
by
.BR "electric-eye status" .
A path failure keeps the event but marks its path uncertain; it is not event
loss. Use
.BR "electric-eye noise" ,
.B ignore_paths
and careful ring sizing to tune a busy host. Retention and disk headroom are
reported by
.BR "electric-eye db status" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR electric-eye (1),
.BR systemd.exec (5),
.BR systemd.resource-control (5)
